WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.570
Film Courage: Questions one should ask themselves
about writing a TV show?

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.310
Peter Russell, script doctor and screenwriter:
Okay, there are nine questions I always ask

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.519
when I’m first talking to someone about
a TV show.

00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:12.570
First of all give us a great world, we need
a great world because we are going to be in

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.750
for 150 episodes.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.710
So what is a great world?

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:21.401
I would do an exercise where you give me a
great world and don’t tell me it’s New

00:00:21.401 --> 00:00:22.401
York City.

00:00:22.401 --> 00:00:26.330
I want to know a specific neighborhood.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.390
What the great world?

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.519
Because great television is always about a
great world.

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:35.480
If you want to talk about New York then give
me a specific world like there’s a show

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.340
called BILLIONS and this show is brilliantly
written, it’s probably the best television

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.950
out there right now and it’s all about billionaires
how they make their money and how they live

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.289
their fascinating lives.

00:00:47.289 --> 00:00:49.940
Another fascinating one is THE ASSASSINATION
OF GIANNI VERSACE.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:57.360
That’s the world of Gianni Versace fashion
world in Miami and then this guy who became

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.679
a his world (I’ve never seen it before).

00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:05.159
So CARBON BLACK is this really cool sci-fi
show.

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:11.362
It’s about a cool future where people can
change bodies so they have sleeves as they

00:01:11.362 --> 00:01:12.362
call it.

00:01:12.362 --> 00:01:16.760
So your world, you want to ask yourself what’s
the world of your show?

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:22.320
ATLANTA is probably the coolest show on television
right now and it’s all about this African

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:30.340
American milieu of middle class (lower middle-class)
Atlanta and this sort of slacker guy in it

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.380
and how he’s going to survive in this slacker
world.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.590
Never seen it before.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:42.979
STRANGER THINGS, that the world of the eighties
and that’s big nostalgia for kids that are

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:44.950
20 right now.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:50.899
People on their bikes without helmets, television
sets that were tubes with rabbit ears.

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:54.520
So the world there is absolutely about nostalgia.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.680
So big question, what’s your world?

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.360
That’s what we want to see.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.030
Fantasy continues to be big.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:07.810
I hate it but zombie is a world that just
continues to be big.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:14.290
Vampires romance, black comedy world (SHAMELESS),
white trash.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:19.260
So worlds are extraordinarily important in
your show.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:30.010
The next question I ask is do you have a theme
that matters.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.210
What do you want o say about the world in
your television show?

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:42.700
For instance a show like SONS OF ANARCHY which
is a great world, a world of motorcycles show.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.090
The theme is if you have a bad mom or dad,
you’re going to be screwed.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:52.600
You need to have a good parent, you need to
be mentored well (he’s not).

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:59.050
And that means the entire show fails because
he doesn’t have a good mom and dad.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:02.470
His dad’s dead and his mom’s horrible.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:07.490
And so this truth about life is probably what
hipsters ignore when they are writing, like

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.490
“Ahhh!

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.490
I want to write this story!”

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:10.490
I call it the.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:15.060
It’s going to be full of motorcycles and
violence and razor blades and people smoking.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.540
And I’m like “Okay, great.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.420
Ten minutes, I’ll love it.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.410
Then what’s the truth about life?

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:24.850
You’re saying Kurt Sutter (SONS OF ANARCHY)
is brilliant writer why?

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:30.790
Because he’s got a theme that a hero that
drives a motorcycle, who is tough, who does

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:36.310
all the things you say you want to see, the
violence, the motorcycles, all that stuff.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:41.830
But every episode is about this guy struggling
with the fact that he doesn’t have a dad.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:47.410
How do I live my life without a dad or a mom
and he never figures it out.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:52.240
And he’s screwed because of some theme answers
the questions.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.300
In ORANGE IS THE NEW BLACK which is a brilliant
show, what’s the theme?

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.730
Well you better know yourself.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.010
Piper doesn’t know if she likes girls or
boys?

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.290
And the whole show is…Do I like guys?

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.740
Do I like girls?

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:04.900
Who am I?

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.710
You tell me who I am.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:07.710
So I don’t know.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.070
So that’s a great theme too.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:15.150
DOWNTON ABBEY has a theme which is the lower
class is rising and we have to understand

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.230
how it’s rising.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.880
That’s the theme of every episode of DOWNTON
ABBEY.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.620
So theme is important.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:29.530
In THE WIRE which is probably the intellectual's
wet dream of a show (THE WIRE), David Simon

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:36.230
in story bible and that’s something you
should also have when you are pitching a story,

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.250
his message is this.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.590
His story (I’m just going to read this because
this is on the front page of the story bible),

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:48.480
this story is about the human condition, it’s
not just a police show, it’s a show about

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:56.320
how we have taken the urban underclass and
have jailed them and tormented them and teared

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.930
them apart in our stupid.

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:10.060
That’s his theme and it’s a social theme
and that motivates every one of those episodes.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.820
So if you don’t have that and it works even
in a sitcom, you’re not going to have a

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.100
show that works.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.510
So what are you passionate about, what do
you believe in, I ask my students this sometimes,

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:21.510
what do you believe in?

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:22.620
They are like “I don’t know?

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.620
It’s important.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:24.620
I’m going to write a hip story.”

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:25.620
I’m like “No you’re not.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.090
You are going to bore us.”

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:30.730
The next question is why do we want to watch
the show every week?

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:34.920
Okay, every week we’ve got to watch a show,
a hundred episodes.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.940
So you need a great question.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:40.370
In BREAKING BAD what’s the great hook question
every week?

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:43.520
How bad is ‘How bad is Walter going to break?

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:44.860
What’s he going to do?

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:46.260
Oh God, he’s going to get worse.”

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.060
MAKING OF A “Hey did he do it or not?

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.970
That’s a pretty good question.”

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.200
Every week we get that.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.440
ORANGE IS THE NEW BLACK, is Piper ever going
to find out if she likes girls or boys?

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.690
Maybe she’s not going to?

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:01.440
But this question continues, it’s the hook,
it’s the great question.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.440
MR.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.450
ROBOT, can Elliot take down the big corporation?

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:09.290
Okay, GAME OF THRONES which family is going
to win?

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.040
Every episode is about that?

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.870
And what’s the theme of GAME OF THRONES
by the way because it’s kind of important?

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:20.940
The theme is Do you have to be evil to be
a great leader?

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:26.360
That’s the whole question for every episode,
right?

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.480
So that’s an important question.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.150
So the next one is genre, let’s now go into
that because I teach that and it’s a structural

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.180
thing but you need to teach that but you need
to stick in your genre.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:37.889
What parents in your show like?

00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:41.390
In television if you’re going to write a
TV show you’ve got to know what are the

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:43.010
parents of the show?

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.430
What shows are like this that you’ve seen
before?

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.580
And my hips students are like “Ahh, Peter
I don’t want to, my show is like nothing

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:49.580
else ever on.”

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.260
And it’s like well okay, then it’s not
going to get on.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:57.580
Even if you want it to be completely different
from everything, you’ve got to understand

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:58.830
what you’re reacting to.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.120
What did you hate?

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.600
You’ve got to have parents for the show
and it will help you build your show if you

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:06.600
do.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:28.050
And the next question (and this is super important)…what
is the question that torments the hero for

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:31.230
ever and ever that will never be resolved?

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:40.930
What’s the unsolvable dilemma that will
never every be solved?

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:51.720
And that is (in MAD MEN) I am unlovable and
the things I do to make myself lovable make

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.560
me so uncomfortable that I run away.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.650
Don Draper seduces woman after woman after
woman but every time they fall in love with

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:04.919
them (they always do), he runs away because
he hasn’t solved his basic dilemma which

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:06.410
is “I’m unlovable.”

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:11.820
Okay so that is the unsolvable dilemma.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:16.550
Even in BUFFY THE VAMPIRE SLAYER it’s “I
just want to be alpha bitch and be in high

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:17.770
school and be popular.”

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:19.020
No, you’re a vampire slayer.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:20.400
“I don’t want to be a vampire slayer.”

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.570
A hundred and fifty episodes of that unsolvable
dilemma.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:31.520
BREAKING BAD, Walt’s dying, he’s got to
take care of his family, how does he do it,

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.360
he becomes a drug dealer and it destroys his
family.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:41.580
So the thing that makes him want to take care
of his family, the way he does it, destroys

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.400
his family.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:49.810
TRUE DETECTIVE, Russ knows the world is meaningless,
Marty believes there is a lot of meaning in

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.740
the world, how do they resolve that?

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:55.520
Well they do in the end because remember it’s
a miniseries so in the end it’s like a movie,

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.650
so in the end the do both believe the world
is a great place.

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.960
But that’s not classic TV, okay?

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:07.420
So will Walt protect his family or destroy
it?

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:09.650
How can good prevail in a world that is run
by the strongest?

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:11.530
That’s GAME OF THRONES.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:17.020
How can we have a world where there is a good
leader when it’s the worst people who always

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:19.430
win, right?

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:20.490
Unsolvable dilemma.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:26.070
Maybe in the end the two people with the Ikea
rugs on the back of their cart do.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:30.220
But for the whole 7 to 8 years they don’t
have it.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:34.400
So what is the irreconcilable conflict in
your show?

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:42.740
What is the dilemma that will never be resolved
that will keep people coming back and obviously

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:45.310
that has to do with their core wounds.

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:48.960
So the next question is “What’s their
big core wound in your hero?

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.900
How does that work in your television show?

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:58.290
And it’s always about that irreconcilable
dilemma.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:09:59.640
MAD MEN “I’m unlovable.”

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.580
Tyrion in GAME OF THRONES “I’m unlovable.”

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.230
Everything he does is because of that.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.440
Walter’s is “I’m weak.”

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.580
And my father used to say “Never corner
a weak man.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:11.580
They’ll turn out to be really strong.”

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.920
Wade in DEADPOOL by the way is “I’m ugly.”

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:20.300
Randall in THIS IS US (which is a great serial
show), Randall is “I’m not part of this

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:21.300
family.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.080
My skin color is different, I’m not really
part of the family.”

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:26.730
Peter in SNEAKY PETE “I’m not part of
the family.”

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:29.610
Jessica Jones in JESSICA JONES “I’m not
safe.

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:30.610
This villain can get me.

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.120
He’ll always get me.”

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:38.000
So core wounds are the nuclear reactors to
your show.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.190
They really are and they have to happen.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:44.730
Daenerys core wound in GAME OF THRONES is
“I’m powerless.”

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:50.370
And we see the entire first season for Daenerys
she starts out as this little slave for her

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:51.370
brother.

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:55.670
She’s give to this warrior as an object.

00:10:55.670 --> 00:11:01.660
By the end of the season she has walked through
fire and hatched dragon wings so her powerlessness

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:07.680
is beginning to go away but it continued through
the entire run of GAME OF THRONES “I’m

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:08.680
powerless.”

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:13.490
Is she getting powerful, she’s getting to
but it’s going to take a long time.

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:17.540
So these are just the beginnings of the questions
that I would ask.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:22.850
There are many others but this is a start
when you are writing television.

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:28.390
And they are different from film but they
do start, when I would start talking to someone

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:30.820
about their TV show, these are the questions
I would ask them at the beginning.

